<h2>Overlays - general informations</h2>

<p>
    Overlays, also called bezels, are images that are placed around the game screen to either add decoration to
    the parts of the screen that would otherwise be black, or give the impression that the game is run in a
    period-accurate setting (like an old school TV border).
</p>

<p>
    Historically, in arcade cabinets, the screen was encased in a wooden or plastic box, behind a plexiglass
    panel, and to hide the screen border and make the cabinet more attractive, some images would be placed on
    the plexiglass: these are called <strong>bezels</strong>. Over time, they would disappear with generic
    cabinets (like the famous Sega Astro City) mostly replacing custom ones.
</p>

<p>
    In very old games, especially in black & white (most notably Space Invaders but it was far from the only one)
    developers added color by simply sticking semi-transparent tape on the screen: this would make some parts
    always of the same color, like the lines of invaders in Space Invaders, or the bricks in Breakout: these are
    called <strong>overlays</strong>. Sometimes they were used to display decorative images, like the moon in some
    versions of Space Invaders, or even part of the game, like Dark Planet 3D.
</p>

<p>
    RetroArch, particularly on mobile devices, displays images atop the game to show the controls (d-pad and
    buttons) on a touch screen. This system is also used to display a bezel around the game screen and overlays
    above it, but I'm not sure that it was its initial purpose.
</p>

<p>
    Mame is more concerned with reproducing the full experience of an arcade cabinet, and their bezel system
    is much more complex, allowing parts that can move or change depending on the game action (like something
    lighting up), interactive elements (like special switches), etc. However, these complex bezels are rare and
    they usually only display a single image.
</p>